"""Original fictional cost, cash and feasibility arithmetic."""


def build(c, m):
    return c(80, 'Business Models Costing and Cash Flow', 'Business Models\nCosting and\nCash Flow',
        'Calculate elementary costs, contribution and cash movements for a fictional enterprise, stating assumptions and limits without providing investment or tax advice.', [
        m('Separate costs revenue and contribution', 'Calculate an elementary unit contribution and explain what it does not cover.', [
            'A financial model describes assumptions about how an enterprise earns and uses resources. Start by identifying the period, unit of service and costs included. Fixed costs remain unchanged within the stated operating range, while variable costs change with the number of units delivered. The distinction depends on the case assumptions; real costs can be mixed or change in steps. General business guidance supports separating startup and recurring costs. This exercise uses invented units of money and does not apply Nigerian tax, employment or accounting rules, which require separate qualified verification. [S61]',
            'Suppose the invented repair service charges 50 units for each completed job and incurs 30 units of variable cost per job. Its contribution is 20 units per job before fixed costs. At ten jobs, revenue is 500, variable cost is 300 and total contribution is 200. Contribution is not the same as net profit, available cash or social benefit. If fixed operating costs for the period are 120, the simplified surplus is 80, before any omitted items. Show the arithmetic and assumptions instead of attaching an unexplained profitability label to the sales total. [S61]',
            'A mission-related activity still needs resources. If supported learning takes staff time or accessible collection requires transport, record its expected cost rather than treating it as a charitable extra that can never affect viability. A sector scoping review describes the coexistence of income and purpose in varied enterprises, but does not establish a universal cost structure. Use that context to ask whether the fictional model funds the service it promises. Do not infer that one study supplies the price, margin or staffing standard for a new local venture. [R72]',
            'Prepare a short cost note identifying included items, omissions and the operating range. Record where an estimate came from in the supplied case and what would need checking before a real decision. A positive simplified surplus can coexist with unpaid obligations, delayed receipts or an unaffordable offer. Foundation enterprise learning requires recognising those boundaries as well as performing arithmetic. The learner should explain the calculation in ordinary language, identify at least one sensitivity and avoid presenting a classroom estimate as audited accounts, a financing recommendation or proof of an enterprise\'s future success. [S62]'],
            'An invented service charges 50 per job, has variable costs of 30 and period fixed costs of 120. Ten jobs are completed. A draft calls the 500 revenue profit and ignores supported-learning costs.',
            'Calculate revenue, variable cost, contribution and simplified surplus. State an omission and explain why cash and social benefit remain separate questions.',
            'Revenue 500 less variable cost 300 gives contribution 200; less fixed costs 120 gives a simplified surplus of 80 before omissions. Revenue is not profit or cash.', ['S61', 'R72', 'S62'], [
                ('A cost model needs a period and unit.', 'What should the fictional repair calculation define first?', 'The period, service unit and included costs.', ['A guaranteed future profit.', 'An assumed tax exemption.', 'Only the venture name.'], 'Scope and units make the arithmetic interpretable.'),
                ('Unit contribution excludes fixed costs.', 'Price is 50 and variable cost is 30 per invented job. What is contribution per job?', '20 units before fixed costs.', ['50 units of net profit.', '30 units of cash automatically received.', '80 units per job.'], 'Subtract variable cost from price; fixed costs remain to be covered.'),
                ('Revenue is not simplified surplus.', 'Ten invented jobs yield revenue 500, variable cost 300 and fixed costs 120. What is the simplified surplus?', '80 units before omitted items.', ['500 units.', '200 units after all possible costs.', 'A guaranteed cash balance of 80.'], 'Subtract the supplied costs while keeping omissions and cash timing explicit.'),
                ('Mission costs belong in feasibility.', 'An invented supported-learning activity uses staff time. How should it be treated?', 'Include or explicitly estimate its resource cost.', ['Assume it is always free.', 'Hide it to improve the margin.', 'Count it only after promising expansion.'], 'A mission promise must be supported by operating resources.'),
                ('Elementary calculations do not confer professional assurance.', 'How should the fictional cost note describe its result?', 'An assumption-based classroom calculation with stated omissions.', ['Audited accounts.', 'Personal investment advice.', 'Proof of future enterprise success.'], 'The exercise teaches reasoning without professional or predictive assurance.')]),
        m('Distinguish cash timing from apparent profit', 'Calculate a simple closing cash balance and identify an operating shortfall.', [
            'Cash flow records when money is actually received and paid, rather than merely when a sale is described. An enterprise can show an expected surplus but lack cash when a bill falls due. Record the opening balance, receipts expected within the period and payments due within the same period. General planning guidance includes cash projections, but a forecast remains an assumption. For this invented case, keep promised receipts separate from money available now, and do not assume a late-paying customer has already financed the work that must be completed first. [S61]',
            'Suppose opening cash is 100 units, confirmed receipts in the period are 80 and payments due are 230. Closing cash is 100 plus 80 minus 230, giving a shortfall of 50. An invoice expected next period cannot be added to this period merely to remove the gap. The shortfall identifies a timing problem that needs a bounded response, such as checking a staged delivery or a different payment schedule in the fictional proposal. It does not authorise the learner to borrow, delay lawful wages or obtain personal funds from classmates. [S61]',
            'Distinguish financing from trading income. A loan creates an obligation, while an equity contribution may affect ownership and a restricted grant may have conditions. The measurement-adoption study reports associations between financing and organisational practices in one observational setting. Those associations do not establish which funding source a particular venture should choose or what return it will earn. In this course, compare the invented terms and their implications for cash, control and mission. Real credit, investment and legal decisions require qualified advice and verified authority beyond a classroom model. [R73]',
            'Build a short dated cash schedule and explain which assumptions would make the position better or worse. Include a delayed-receipt scenario and keep a record of unpaid commitments so the schedule does not create a false impression of available resources. State the action owner and the condition that must be resolved before a proposed activity proceeds. A credible learning output can recommend pausing an invented launch when timing is unresolved. It should not conceal a gap inside optimistic sales, describe borrowed money as profit or promise that financial confidence replaces evidence of actual receipts. [S62]'],
            'An invented venture opens with 100, receives 80 and owes 230 this period. A 200-unit invoice falls due next period. The team reports 150 closing cash by counting the future invoice early.',
            'Calculate the correct period balance, distinguish the future invoice and propose two bounded ways to examine the timing gap without taking real financial action.',
            'The current period has a 50-unit shortfall. The future invoice belongs to its expected receipt period; examine timing or scale before any authorised real commitment.', ['S61', 'R73', 'S62'], [
                ('Cash flow concerns receipt and payment timing.', 'Which belongs in the fictional current-period receipts?', 'Money expected to arrive within that period under the stated assumptions.', ['Every invoice regardless of due date.', 'An unapproved loan treated as received.', 'An optimistic annual sales total.'], 'The schedule must match cash movements to the chosen period.'),
                ('A future receipt cannot remove a present gap automatically.', 'Opening cash 100 plus receipts 80 less payments 230 gives what balance?', 'A shortfall of 50 units.', ['A surplus of 150 by adding next period\'s invoice.', 'A profit of 230.', 'A guaranteed zero balance.'], 'The supplied current-period arithmetic is 100 + 80 - 230.'),
                ('Borrowing is not trading profit.', 'What does a fictional loan receipt also create?', 'A repayment obligation under its terms.', ['Guaranteed net profit.', 'No future cash implications.', 'An automatic mission benefit.'], 'Financing inflow and earned surplus are different categories.'),
                ('Observed financing associations are not personal recommendations.', 'How should the Slovenian measurement study inform a finance discussion?', 'As contextual observational evidence with limits, not a funding prescription.', ['As proof that all enterprises should choose one source.', 'As an investment return guarantee.', 'As a Nigerian lending rule.'], 'Its design and outcome do not establish a universally best financing decision.'),
                ('An unresolved shortfall can justify a pause.', 'Which fictional launch decision is proportionate?', 'Pause or modify the plan pending an authorised solution to timing.', ['Borrow real money through coursework.', 'Conceal unpaid commitments.', 'Count unreceived money as cash.'], 'A bounded response recognises the gap without unauthorised financial action.')]),
        m('Use break-even and sensitivity honestly', 'Calculate a simplified break-even point and examine changes in assumptions.', [
            'A break-even calculation asks how many units cover the supplied fixed and variable costs under a particular set of assumptions. With a positive contribution, divide period fixed costs by contribution per unit. The result is a simplified operating threshold, not a prediction that customers will buy that quantity. General planning guidance uses this calculation to examine feasibility. State the unit, period and costs included, and explain that capacity, quality, access and cash timing can still make a plan unworkable even when the arithmetic produces a neat number. [S61]',
            'For the invented repair service, fixed costs of 120 and contribution of 20 imply six jobs to break even in the period. If variable cost rises from 30 to 35 while price remains 50, contribution falls to 15 and break-even rises to eight jobs. If price is no higher than variable cost, each extra job fails to provide a positive contribution toward fixed costs; the simple positive-contribution formula no longer gives a feasible threshold. These examples use invented money units and omit tax and financing details. Show each change rather than hiding it inside a single optimistic scenario. [S61]',
            'Compare the threshold with a realistic supplied capacity and a bounded demand assumption. A case that can safely deliver five jobs cannot satisfy an eight-job threshold merely by changing the spreadsheet. Raising price may exclude intended users, while cutting support time may weaken the mission. Sector research describes contextual arrangements rather than a guaranteed formula for balancing those pressures. Record which trade-off the fictional team would need to examine and whose perspective is missing. Do not assume that financial break-even automatically establishes fairness, decent work or a positive social outcome. [R72]',
            'Finish with a small scenario table in words or numbers: base assumptions, one adverse change and a condition for reconsideration. Separate the calculated threshold from the observation needed to assess demand, and check the cash schedule independently. Proportionate impact guidance supports keeping the purpose visible when using evidence for decisions. The learner recommendation should explain whether the invented plan needs a changed design, more information or a pause. It must not promise returns, advise a personal investment or label a classroom break-even calculation as a complete business valuation. [S64]'],
            'An invented service has fixed costs 120, price 50 and variable cost 30. Variable cost may rise to 35, while safe capacity is five jobs per period. The team claims six jobs guarantees both profit and social impact.',
            'Calculate both thresholds, compare them with capacity and explain one mission trade-off and one cash question that remain.',
            'Break-even is six jobs in the base case and eight after the cost rise; both exceed the supplied five-job capacity. Neither threshold proves demand, cash sufficiency or impact.', ['S61', 'R72', 'S64'], [
                ('Break-even is conditional arithmetic rather than a sales forecast.', 'What does the fictional six-job threshold mean?', 'Six jobs cover supplied costs under the base assumptions.', ['Six customers are guaranteed to buy.', 'Cash timing can be ignored.', 'Every social outcome is achieved.'], 'The threshold depends on assumptions and does not demonstrate demand.'),
                ('Higher variable cost lowers contribution.', 'Variable cost rises to 35 with price 50 and fixed costs 120. What is the new threshold?', 'Eight jobs.', ['Six jobs unchanged.', 'Three jobs.', 'A guaranteed profit at every volume.'], 'Contribution is 15, and 120 divided by 15 is eight.'),
                ('Non-positive contribution changes the feasibility question.', 'What if the invented price is no higher than variable cost?', 'Additional jobs do not provide positive contribution toward fixed costs.', ['The usual formula guarantees an attainable threshold.', 'Every sale necessarily increases surplus.', 'Fixed costs cease to exist.'], 'A positive-contribution threshold cannot be assumed in that case.'),
                ('Capacity is separate from a calculated threshold.', 'Safe fictional capacity is five jobs while break-even is eight. What follows?', 'The supplied operating design cannot meet that threshold as stated.', ['Change the reported capacity without evidence.', 'Guarantee success from the calculation.', 'Ignore service quality.'], 'A spreadsheet threshold does not create operating capacity.'),
                ('Financial viability and mission outcomes need distinct evidence.', 'What remains after the invented venture reaches break-even?', 'Questions about access, cash, work conditions and social outcomes.', ['Automatic proof of equitable impact.', 'No further mission review.', 'A complete business valuation.'], 'Covering supplied costs does not settle the venture\'s wider purpose.')])])
